    </Number>
    <Label Name="Title" Style="Heading" Text="Make a Rainbow" Pin="TopRight" OffsetX="16" OffsetY="16" WrapWidth="400" Backdrop="true" />
    <Label Name="Description" Style="Explanation" DecimalsToShow="1" Text="White light is really all the colors mixed together. Glass bends each color by a different amount, violet the most and red the least, so a prism pulls them apart into a rainbow.\n\nTurn the prism by its top corner, or drag it. Aim the lamp with the knob in front of it, or move the lamp. At one angle the colors bend the least, and there they are brightest. Turn too far and the light can't get out where it first meets a side: it bounces off the inside like off a mirror and leaves through the next side, and there the colors bend back together, almost white again.\n\nThis prism is heavy flint glass, which spreads the colors more than window glass. Red to violet: [spread]°.\n\nIsaac Newton did this in 1666 with sunlight through a hole in his shutter, and was the first to say that the colors were in the white light all along." Pin="TopRight" OffsetX="16" OffsetY="64" WrapWidth="400" Backdrop="true">
      <Dependency Name="spread" />
    </Label>
  </Figures>
</Drawing>